## What Update 57 took from us: nothing, and that is correct

The release note says plainly: **"No outside PR landed this cycle."** Our queue
agrees -- all eleven PRs 117 through 134 are CLOSED and none is open, because
they all went in at U56.

**The question worth asking instead is whether the abort disturbed what U56 DID
take.** An aborted Update followed by a withheld-block purge is exactly the
event that drops something quietly. `6cd2ca1b` being an ancestor makes this
exact rather than a sampling probe: take the files each PR branch touched and
ask whether U57 moved any of them.

    PR   branch                                  files  moved in U57
    117  compiler-hosted-kind-check-compact          2   1
    118  zig-plug-memory-builtins                    2   0
    119  zig-plug-closure-param-namespace            2   0
    127  mcopy-type-variant-tag                      1   0
    128  zig-plug-empty-slice-and-ll-copy            1   0
    129  wgsl-firefox-naga                          37   0
    130  zig-plug-real-int-conversions               3   0
    131  zig-address-of-literal-pr                  45   1
    132  cite-hoist-pr                              47   1
    133  crlf-foreword-pr                           16   1
    134  binding-lookup-duplicate-key                1   1

**All five "moved" files are the same file: `compiler-backlog.md`.** Every CODE
file our landed PRs touched is byte-identical between U56 and U57. The abort
disturbed none of our work.

That the only movement is in the register is the expected shape -- prose churns
every Update, which is why the probe is run on files rather than on text.

## COMPILER-66 is fixed, and it is the open half of PR 134

The register's own rule is that a closed entry is DELETED and a real gap is
never quietly dropped, so a row vanishing is ambiguous on its face. Six rows
vanished (COMPILER-9, 20, 43, 66, 68, 69) and one arrived (COMPILER-72).

COMPILER-66 was ours and still open, so it is the one to verify rather than
accept. It is a real fix, in `IR/LoweringTypes.codex`:

    -  compare-binding-names (a) (b) = text-compare (a.name) (b.name)
    +  compare-binding-names (a) (b) =
    +   let by-name = text-compare (a.name) (b.name)
    +   in if by-name /= 0 then by-name
    +   else binding-shape-rank (a.bound-type) - binding-shape-rank (b.bound-type)

with `binding-shape-rank` ranking SumTy/RecordTy 0, ForAllTy/ForAllEff 2, and
everything else 1. **They made the COMPARATOR total rather than making the sort
stable.** `all-bindings` still holds two differing entries per definition, but
the two no longer compare equal, so the unstable qsort cannot order them by
accident and the lower-bound `bsearch-text-pos` lands on the peeled entry every
time.

That closes an owed item on our side: the register recorded that they would
want our peak-linear-memory arm **if a stable sort became the repair**. It did
not become the repair, so that instrument is not needed.

## A new warning that measures our own recurring mistake

`CDX2097 PartialApplicationUnused` is new, and the whole +148 of
`Types/TypeCheckerInference.codex` is its implementation:

  A let binds a partial application of a named function and nothing reads the
  binding, so the call is never completed and its effects never happen.

**That is the harness-fidelity failure mode, given a diagnostic.**
`check-chapter` went 5 parameters to 9 at U54 and every harness kept passing
five; under-application evaluates to a function value and the type error lands
phases later against whatever consumed it. Upstream now warns at the binding.

Untested by us as yet: whether it fires on our harnesses, and whether it fires
anywhere in upstream's own tree.

